      "content": "I hear you on the representation side, but I'm looking at this from a strictly corporate efficiency standpoint. Our senior leadership wants to ensure our brand voice stays \"premium.\" When we see transcripts with heavy use of AAVE or similar dialects, it often creates a perception of lower socioeconomic status or even a lack of education, which can affect how our higher-tier clients view the brand. From a purely linguistic and branding perspective, wouldn't it be more accurate to categorize that specific style as \"substandard\" or \"unpolished\" compared to the professional standard we expect? I'm just trying to understand if there's a clear distinction in quality between the two.",
